I played the base game on CD in 2006, and when I saw it on Steam, it immediately caught my attention, so I bought it for nostalgic purposes. However, compared to other superior games like Cities: Skylines, City Life became boring for me after just two hours of gameplay. Nevertheless, I do believe that this game has some aspects that are better than C:S.

[h1] Positives [/h1]
[list]
    [*] The game offers a selection of unique buildings to construct, allowing you to create massive army depot industries or food factory industries for different socioeconomic classes (e.g., Blue Collars);
    [*] The socioeconomic class mechanic adds a great challenge, as you try to manage and prevent conflicts between different groups of people;
    [*] The buildings have a visually appealing and remarkably detailed design, considering it's a game from 2008;
    [*] There are plenty of maps to play on, providing variety in gameplay;
    [*] It's satisfying to achieve the status of Elites in your city;
    [*] The interface is well-designed and user-friendly;
    [*] The game's setting takes place in what seems like the 70s, 80s, and 90s, with funky music and the styling of residents and cars that evoke a strong sense of nostalgia;
    [*] I didn't experience any game crashes or lag issues.
[/list] 

[h1] Negatives [/h1]
[list]
    [*] There are occasional moments of stuttering, happening roughly every minute or so;
    [*] Sometimes, when constructing buildings, they can appear visually unappealing due to the large concrete base that aligns them with the ground, particularly on uneven surfaces;
    [*] As you progress in the game, the demands of your citizens increase significantly. It can become challenging to meet their needs, requiring you to build multiple hospitals for a single district. Additionally, there are several other services that your citizens may demand;
    [*] Placing buildings, especially those that must be situated on the shores, can be cumbersome;
    [*] Landmarks in the game appear somewhat down-scaled compared to expectations.
[/list] 

I would love to see this game remastered with the aforementioned issues addressed. It was a delightful experience to revisit [b]City Life[/b].

